Q: Truthy modulo vs explicit comparisons: Which Gregorian leap year solution is better Python? (learning Python) A: <p>Personally, both solutions seem unintuitive, but the intended solution is slightly more explicit. If I had to write this program, I'd probably go at it from the other side:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>year = int(input(&quot;Enter a year: &quot;))
if year &lt; 1582:
    print(&quot;Not within the Gregorian calendar period&quot;)
elif year % 400 == 0:
    print(&quot;Leap year&quot;)
elif year % 100 == 0:
    print(&quot;Common year&quot;)
elif year % 4 == 0:
    print(&quot;Leap year&quot;)
else:
    print(&quot;Common year&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>I feel it is much more intuitive to test for divisibility, instead of indivisibility.</p>
